.TH DANBY-SCHRODINGER 1
.SH NAME
danby-schrodinger \- shoot Schrodinger's equation and hunt for an eigenvalue
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B danby/schrodinger
.SH MODEL
The time-independent Schrodinger equation in one dimension, in units
where h-bar is 1:
.PP
.EX
psi'' = 2 m (V(x) - E) psi,   V(x) = k x\ep2 / 2 + lambda x\ep4
.EE
.PP
The well is symmetric about the origin, so every solution is either even
or odd there, and the march starts at
.I x
= 0 from one or the other \(em
.I psi
= 1,
.I psi'
= 0 for an even state,
.I psi
= 0,
.I psi'
= 1 for an odd one \(em and runs outward to
.I x
= 5.
.PP
Almost every energy is wrong, and says so.  A general solution of that
equation contains the growing exponential as well as the decaying one,
so unless the two are in exactly the proportion that kills the growing
part,
.I psi
runs away and the plot's own axis rescales to hold it.  Only at an
eigenvalue does the tail stay down.  That is the exercise: turn the
energy slider until it does.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Danby/schrodinger
is a
.IR danbylab (2)
script and nothing else \(em the equations, the parameter ranges, the
two curves and the little diagram are all one string in
.BR /appl/danby/schrodinger.b .
.PP
It belongs to that library's
.B mechanism
family, whose independent variable is called
.I t
and whose graph's own x axis is therefore labelled
.BR time .
Here it is
.IR position .
That family was chosen because it is the only one that integrates with
.IR numerics (2)'s
Runge-Kutta stepper
.I and
plots its two observables on an axis that goes below zero, which is what
a wave function needs; the
.B profile
family, which marches outward in a radius and would otherwise have been
the obvious home, holds every state at or above zero \(em correct for
the stellar densities it was written for, fatal for anything that
oscillates through the axis.
.SS The two curves
.B Amplitude
is
.I psi
and
.B probability
is
.IR "psi squared" ,
the probability density.  They share one axis.  At an eigenvalue both
are of order 1 and the picture is the textbook one: a hump with
.I n
nodes under a smooth density.  At any other energy the density is the
square of a number that has run away, so it dwarfs everything and the
axis goes with it \(em which is the loudest possible way of saying that
this energy is not one.
.SS The diagram
Left of the graph is the physical picture, and it holds still: the well
.IR V ( x )
as a curve with a wall on each side, the energy
.I E
as a straight line drawn across it, and one marker.
.PP
Nothing in it is a particle, and nothing in it is moving.  This is the
time-INDEPENDENT equation: it has no time in it, the solution is a
standing shape, and there is nothing to animate.  What the marker shows
is how far the integration has got \(em it is the solver's own position,
not anybody's.  It rides along the energy line because that is the
energy the shot is being taken at.
.PP
The march runs outward from the centre only, since a symmetric well's
solutions are even or odd and the other half follows from parity, so the
marker crosses the right half and stops.  The well is drawn whole
because the WELL is whole; it is only the integration that is done once.
.PP
Where the energy line cuts the wall is the classical turning point.
Inside it the wave oscillates and outside it decays, which is what the
graph beside it shows happening.
.PP
The well is drawn to seventeen samples and clipped into \-1.5 to 4.5, so
that a steep well or an upside-down one cannot drag the vertical scale
away and squash the energy line flat against the floor.  Inside that
band the shape is exact.  Drag
.B stiffness
below zero with
.B quartic
up and the single well visibly becomes two with a hump between them.
.SS The parameters
.TP
.B energy
.IR E .
The slider runs \-1 to 4 in steps of 0.025, and lands exactly on 0.5,
1.5, 2.5 and 3.5.
.TP
.B stiffness
.IR k .
Positive for a single well; negative, with
.B quartic
turned up, for a double one.
.TP
.B quartic
.IR lambda ,
the anharmonic term.  Zero for the plain harmonic oscillator.
.TP
.B mass
.IR m .
.TP
.B parity
Below 0.5 the march starts even, above it odd.  It is a switch, not a
quantity; a
.IR danbylab (2)
parameter is a slider, and this is what a slider makes of a switch.
.PP
Changing any of them starts the march again from
.I x
= 0, which is what makes the hunt work: drag the energy, watch the tail.
.SS What to look for
With the defaults \(em
.B stiffness
and
.B mass
both 1,
.B quartic
zero \(em the well is the harmonic oscillator, whose eigenvalues are
exactly
.RI ( n
+ 1/2), so 0.5, 1.5, 2.5 and 3.5, alternating even and odd.  The model
opens on the first of them.  One notch of the slider away from 0.5 the
tail is 3e8 times larger.
.PP
Winding
.B mass
up moves the ground state down as 0.5 sqrt(k/m), which is the same
statement as saying a heavier particle is less confined.
.PP
Winding
.B stiffness
negative and
.B quartic
up bends the well into two, and the single level becomes a pair, one
even and one odd.  In a double well deep enough for it, that pair closes
up as the barrier between the two halves grows, since the splitting is
what tunnelling through the barrier costs \(em but these sliders do not
reach such a well, and it is worth knowing that they do not.  Measured
at four settings along the way, the split goes 0.49 at
.B stiffness
\-1 and
.B quartic
0.15, then 0.79, 0.80 and 0.79 at \-2/0.5, \-3/0.75 and \-4/1: it widens
and then flattens, and never narrows.  The reason is that with h-bar at
1 the barrier here is never tall compared with the level spacing \(em at
the deepest the sliders go it is only 1 high \(em so the pair sits at or
above the top of it and is not tunnelling through anything.  What the
double well demonstrates on these ranges is a broad quartic well's first
two levels, not a tunnelling doublet.
.SS Keys
Space pauses and resumes, period advances one displayed step while
paused, r starts again, and q or Escape quits, as in
.IR danby (1).
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/danby/schrodinger.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby (1),
.IR danbylab (2),
.IR danbylabtest (1),
.IR dynsys (2),
.IR hoc (2),
.IR numerics (2)
.SH BUGS
Shooting outward from the centre is the wrong way to integrate into a
classically forbidden region: out there the growing solution is the
stable one, so roundoff feeds it, and far enough out the computed tail
stops being the wave function at all.  At the far wall this model
actually uses it has not happened yet \(em at
.I x
= 5 all four harmonic tails are within 2% of the analytic wave function
\(em but at
.I x
= 6 the ground state's computed tail is already 2165 times its analytic
value, and the contrast between a right energy and a wrong one has
stopped improving, both ends of it being the same roundoff.  That is why
the wall is at 5.
.PP
.IR Danbylabtest (1)
checks the potential against a formula written out in Limbo, checks that
each parity starts where it should, and checks all four harmonic
eigenvalues with a control one slider notch either side.  It does
.I not
check the anharmonic or double-well cases against anything \(em there is
no closed form for those eigenvalues to check them against \(em and it
does not look at the picture at all.
.PP
There is no normalisation.  The
.B probability
curve is
.I psi
squared for whatever
.I psi
the march happened to start with, not a density that integrates to one,
so its height is arbitrary even when its shape is right.
.PP
The energy slider cannot reach an eigenvalue below \-1, so a well deep
enough to put one there cannot be solved here.  That is the same limit
that keeps a real tunnelling doublet out of reach: it would need a
barrier tall compared with h-bar, and a well that deep puts its own
levels below where the slider stops.
